---
export const title = 'Usage';
export const order = 100;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  <code>wc-form</code> wraps a native <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> and adds what enterprise forms
  otherwise build by hand: an error summary, unsaved-change tracking, a busy state while saving and
  errors from the server. Write the form inside it, in the page:
  <code>&lt;wc-form&gt;&lt;form&gt;…&lt;/form&gt;&lt;/wc-form&gt;</code>. Every Peacock field and
  native control in the form counts.
</p>
<p>
  Submit the form below empty. Instead of submitting, an error summary at the top lists each
  invalid field; its links move focus to the field, and it updates as you fix them. A valid submit
  fires <code>submit</code> with <code>detail.value</code>, the form data as a plain object (a name
  submitted more than once becomes an array). Call <code>preventDefault()</code> to save it
  yourself; otherwise the form submits natively to its <code>action</code>.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <div style='width: 100%; max-width: 400px;'>
    <wc-form id='form-demo'>
      <form style='display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem;'>
        <wc-input label='Name' name='name' required></wc-input>
        <wc-input label='Email' name='email' type='email' required></wc-input>
        <wc-select label='Team' name='team' required>
          <wc-option value='design'>Design</wc-option>
          <wc-option value='engineering'>Engineering</wc-option>
        </wc-select>
        <wc-checkbox label='Accept the terms' name='terms' required></wc-checkbox>
        <div style='display: flex; gap: 0.5rem;'>
          <wc-button type='submit'>Save</wc-button>
          <wc-button type='reset' variant='text'>Reset</wc-button>
        </div>
      </form>
    </wc-form>
  </div>
  <script is:inline>
    document.querySelector('#form-demo').addEventListener('submit', function (event) {
      if (!(event instanceof CustomEvent)) return;
      event.preventDefault();
      myConsole.log('submit: ' + JSON.stringify(event.detail.value));
    });
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>

<p>
  With <code>no-summary</code> the summary is off, and an invalid submit moves focus to the first
  invalid field, which shows its message. <code>validate()</code> runs the same check without
  submitting and resolves to whether the form is valid.
</p>
